<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02034</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4fbbcbfc-c875-4601-8a9c-7d6f8f6d3a8d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section id="sect-00002"><exercise id="_3bdda842-a202-4cfa-96bf-c80303719329" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00002">What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00003">Correct! 2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2.</para><para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2 – 7.16.4.</para><para id="para-00005">The correct answer is 2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="b9a9a833-8d7c-4a05-be34-ce23d9a12115" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00006">What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00007">Correct! -8. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is -8.</para><para id="para-00008">Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2 – 7.16.4.</para><para id="para-00009">The correct answer is -8. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is -8.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_10697901-1d71-4c1e-8aa3-341cded90fed" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00010">Where is the \(y\)-intercept for the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\) located?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00011">Correct! -4. Substitute 0 for \(x\) and you get \(y=(0−2)^2−8=4−8=-4\).</para><para id="para-00012">Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2 – 7.16.4.</para><para id="para-00013">The correct answer is -4. Substitute 0 for \(x\) and you get \(y=(0−2)^2−8=4−8=-4\).</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_7614b261-dbad-48ee-a025-2f05bc4aa041" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00014">Which of the following is another point on the graph of the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      <para id="para-00015">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00016">\((4,-4)\)</para><para id="para-00017">\((-4,4)\)</para><para id="para-00018">\((4,4)\)</para><para id="para-00019">\((-4,-4)\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00020">Correct! \((4,-4)\). The \(y\)-intercept is 2 horizontal units to the left of the vertical line containing the vertex (the axis of symmetry). Using the symmetry of the graph, another point with the same \(y\)-coordinate is located 2 horizontal units to the right of the vertical line containing the vertex.</para><para id="para-00021">Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.2.</para><para id="para-00022">The correct answer is: \((4,-4)\). The \(y\)-intercept is 2 horizontal units to the left of the vertical line containing the vertex (the axis of symmetry). Using the symmetry of the graph, another point with the same \(y\)-coordinate is located 2 horizontal units to the right of the vertical line containing the vertex.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_51a7d19d-acae-4276-9c8c-dd6ad3831974" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00023">Which graph is a representation of \(y=(x - 6)^2 - 5\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00024">Correct! Yes graph A.</para><para id="para-00025">Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.3 and 7.16.4.</para><para id="para-00026">The correct answer is Yes.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_57ca4658-384a-4e06-88db-22c107551c69" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00006"><para id="para-00027">Without graphing, determine if the vertex of the graph representing \(v\) shows the minimum or maximum value of the function.</para><para id="para-00028">The function \(v\) is defined by \(v(x)=12(x+5)^2−7\).</para>
      <para id="para-00029">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00030">The vertex shows the minimum value.</para><para id="para-00031">The vertex shows the maximum value.</para></problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00032">Correct! The vertex shows the minimum value. The \(a\)-value, or the coefficient of the squared term is positive, so the graph opens upward.This means the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value of the function. The vertex is at \((-5,-7)\). To verify your response, enter a couple of \(x\) -values on either side of -5 into the function. For example, find \(v(-6)\) and \(v(-4)\). Each time, the output is greater than \(v(-5)\).</para><para id="para-00033">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para><para id="para-00034">The correct answer is: The vertex shows the minimum value. The \(a\)-value, or the coefficient of the squared term is positive, so the graph opens upward.This means the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value of the function. The vertex is at \((-5,-7)\). To verify your response, enter a couple of \(x\) -values on either side of -5 into the function. For example, find \(v(-6)\) and \(v(-4)\). Each time, the output is greater than \(v(-5)\).</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_88f0ebcf-8946-42f3-9d92-5ccad7f2a77e" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00007"><para id="para-00035">Choose the equation that matches the graph of parabola D .</para>
      <para id="para-00036">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00037">\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00038">\(y=(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00039">\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00040">\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00007"><para id="para-00041">Correct! \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00042">Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3.</para><para id="para-00043">The correct answer is: \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\).
    </para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_7786bff1-c50b-4411-b3e8-1b0fcf3eded0" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00008"><para id="para-00044">Choose the equation that matches the graph B.</para>
      <para id="para-00045">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00046">\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00047">\(y=(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00048">\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00049">\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00008"><para id="para-00050">Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3.
    </para><para id="para-00051">The correct answer is: \(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\).
    </para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_26117eea-4e73-4f7b-8e19-a0eecac697c2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00009"><para id="para-00052">Choose the equation that matches the graph C.</para>
      <para id="para-00053">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00054">\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00055">\(y=(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00056">\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para id="para-00057">\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00009"><para id="para-00058">Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3.</para><para id="para-00059">The correct answer is: \(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
